post blog-the-dose-you-already-get
author: npc-mina
lesson: the-dose-scale
title: Calm note: the dose you already get
hook: You get a little radiation every day, and so does everyone else. Here is how much.
cover: dial
read: 1
unlock: {"trigger":"lesson_complete","lessonId":"the-dose-scale"}
reactors: npc-ray
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

p:
    If radiation worries you a little, that is fine. A lot of people feel that way. Let's start with something steady: you are already getting a small dose, every day, and so is everyone you know.

h: How it is counted

p:
    A dose is counted in sieverts. Everyday doses are tiny, so they are usually given in millisieverts. One sievert is 1000 millisieverts.

h: An ordinary year

p:
    In the United States the average person gets about 6.2 millisieverts a year. Most of it is natural background radiation.

figure: dial
caption: A dial with its needle resting in the low zone, and the everyday sources round it: sun, rock, grass and water.

quote:
    Most of our average yearly dose comes from natural background radiation.

fact:
    A whole body CT scan is about 10 millisieverts. About 1000 millisieverts in a short time is where people start to become ill.

list:
    Living near a nuclear power station for a year: under 0.01 millisieverts
    Cosmic radiation at sea level for a year: about 0.3 millisieverts
    Radon in an average home for a year: about 2.3 millisieverts

p:
    A number only means something when you put it next to the ones you know. That is all a scale is for.

